<!-- wallet.dsx — the money trust-chain, server-side only. The client renders balances;
     it NEVER computes them. Two balances per the product spec: coins (PURCHASED, durable)
     and bonus (GRANTED by the rewards loop, spent first). Every movement is a ledger row;
     the wallet row is a cached fold the actions keep true.
     NOTE (upstream ledger): the standalone server compiler has no <webhook> tag yet, so
     the RevenueCat receiver is a hosted-lane integration for now; local coin packs are
     credited through the operator-gated admin grant. Sibling actions return void by
     design, so the ensure-wallet fold is inlined per action.

     ── NEITHER BALANCE EXPIRES, AND THAT IS A COMPLIANCE RULE, NOT A PREFERENCE ──────────
     App Store Review Guideline 3.1.1: "Any credits or in-game currencies purchased via
     in-app purchase may not expire." 3.2.1(iii) permits an expiry window only for the
     RENTAL of specific approved content — a coin balance is not that. This document used
     to write a 7-day `expires` onto every granted-bonus ledger row (nothing ever read it,
     so nothing expired — but the column made the promise real enough to ship). The column
     and every write of it are GONE rather than left unimplemented: a capability that can
     expire a balance is one refactor away from expiring a purchased one, and the coin
     packs credit part of their value into `bonus` (`settleOrder` writes `o.bonus`, the
     "+5% free" of a paid pack), so `bonus` is NOT a purely-granted bucket and an expiry on
     it would expire purchased value directly.
     The two balances stay separate because spend ORDER is a real product rule (granted
     first, so a viewer's purchased coins outlive their free ones) and because keeping the
     purchased balance nameable is what makes 3.1.1 provable. Neither expires.
     AND THE COPY AGREES NOW. This paragraph used to hand three Profile lines forward as a
     handover (PLAN.md §6.85); all three are gone. The last of them was the dangerous one
     precisely because it was already unreachable — a second interpolation appended to the
     ledger row's kind, `{{ item.expires == null ? '' : ' · expires in 7 days' }}`, which
     `ledgerRows` had stopped feeding. An unreachable promise is one payload change from a
     live one, so it was deleted rather than left. `npm run verify` now asserts the copy
     beside the behaviour: no display string in Components/** claims an expiry unless it
     NEGATES one, read through the app's own display-point extractor.

     ── RESTORE IS PART OF THE SAME GUIDELINE ────────────────────────────────────────────
     3.1.1 also requires a restore mechanism for restorable purchases. Entitlement here is
     SERVER-SIDE and account-scoped (wallet.vip_until, unlock rows), so a reinstall that
     signs back in already has everything — the real gap is an order that was PAID at the
     provider and never granted here (the app closed between confirm and settle). That is
     `restoreOrders` in store.dsx. The NATIVE store lanes (StoreKit / Play Billing /
     RevenueCat) have no receiver in this template at all — named in store.dsx and in
     PLAN.md §6.86, not silently missing. -->
<server>
  <head>
    <entity as="wallet" ownership="owner">
      <field as="coins" type="integer"/>
      <field as="bonus" type="integer"/>
      <field as="vip_until" type="timestamptz"/>
    </entity>

    <entity as="ledger" ownership="owner">
      <field as="kind" type="text"/>       <!-- coin | bonus -->
      <field as="amount" type="integer"/>  <!-- signed -->
      <field as="source" type="text"/>     <!-- checkin | task | spin | ad | pack | vip | unlock -->
      <!-- `ref` is the thing the movement is ABOUT: an episode id for an unlock, a day for a
           check-in, and — since the settle rewrite — the ORDER ID for a pack/vip grant. That
           last one is load-bearing: policies.local.sql puts a partial unique index on
           (owner_id, kind, source, ref) for source in ('pack','vip'), which makes the ledger
           row itself the exactly-once lock on a payment grant (see store.dsx settleOrder). -->
      <field as="ref" type="text"/>
    </entity>

    <entity as="unlock" ownership="owner">
      <field as="episode" type="uuid"/>
      <field as="show" type="uuid"/>
      <field as="price" type="integer"/>
      <index on="show"/>
      <index on="episode"/>
    </entity>

    <!-- THE PLAYBACK TICKET — a short-lived, per-viewer, per-episode grant that the media
         plane can check without re-deriving entitlement on every byte range.
         WHY A ROW AND NOT A SIGNATURE. A production deployment signs the URL: CloudFront
         `Expires`+`Signature`, a Cloudflare signed-URL token, or a Mux signed playback JWT —
         the edge verifies an HMAC and never talks to the database. A declared action cannot
         do that today: the JSE seam a `<server>` body may call is `data` · `queue` · `secret`
         · declared packages (actions.ts moduleTable) and there is NO crypto/HMAC among them,
         so there is no way to compute a signature in the declared lane. Filed as PLAN.md
         §6.82 (`dsx.module.crypto.hmac` on the server seam). Until it lands, the capability
         is a row id: `gen_random_uuid()` is 122 bits the caller cannot guess, the row carries
         its own expiry, and scripts/serve.mjs checks both. Same security property, one round
         trip more.
         BOUNDED BY CONSTRUCTION: policies.local.sql puts `unique (owner_id, episode)` on it,
         and the action REFRESHES the existing row rather than inserting a second — so the
         table is at most one row per episode a viewer has actually played, the same order as
         `unlock`, and it never grows per play. -->
    <entity as="playticket" ownership="owner">
      <field as="episode" type="uuid"/>
      <field as="expires" type="timestamptz"/>
      <index on="episode"/>
    </entity>

    <action as="walletState">
      const mine = await dsx.module.data.wallet.list({ limit: 1 })
      let w = mine.ok && mine.data.length > 0 ? mine.data[0] : null
      if (w == null) {
        // TWO CONCURRENT FIRST CALLS USED TO CREATE TWO WALLETS and the balance split
        // between them (`list({limit:1})` then returns whichever row Postgres feels like).
        // `unique (owner_id)` in policies.local.sql makes the second insert fail; the loser
        // re-reads the winner's row instead of throwing. There is no transaction seam in a
        // declared action (PLAN.md §6.38), so the constraint IS the lock — every
        // read-then-write guard in this document is written this way.
        const made = await dsx.module.data.wallet.create({ coins: 0, bonus: 0 })
        if (made.ok) {
          w = made.data
        } else {
          const again = await dsx.module.data.wallet.list({ limit: 1 })
          w = again.ok && again.data.length > 0 ? again.data[0] : null
        }
        if (w == null) { throw { reason: 'unavailable', message: 'wallet unavailable' } }
      }
      // 100 is the ceiling `list` actually honours; a viewer past it would see the oldest
      // unlocks fall out of this set, so the flag travels with it and the UI says so rather
      // than quietly re-locking an episode the viewer paid for
      const unlocks = await dsx.module.data.unlock.list({ limit: 100 })
      const vip = w.vip_until != null && new Date(w.vip_until).getTime() > Date.now()
      return {
        coins: w.coins, bonus: w.bonus, vip: vip, vipUntil: w.vip_until,
        unlocked: (unlocks.ok ? unlocks.data : []).map(u => u.episode),
        unlockedCapped: unlocks.ok && unlocks.data.length >= 100
      }
    </action>

    <!-- THE ENTITLED SOURCE. `/catalog/show/:id` is public and now returns a playable URL
         only for episodes that are free to everyone; everything else asks HERE, with a
         token, and the answer is a URL that stops working in five minutes.

         The check is the SAME THREE FACTS unlockEpisode charges on — free window, VIP,
         an unlock row — deliberately spelled the same way, because two entitlement rules
         that must agree and are written differently is how a paying VIP ends up staring at
         a padlock (the audit's G8, one layer up in the client). If one of them changes,
         both sites change together or the gate is wrong in one direction or the other.

         WHAT A PRODUCTION CDN DOES INSTEAD. It signs. This action would return
         `https://cdn.example.com/ep/<id>/master.m3u8?Expires=…&Signature=…` (CloudFront),
         or a Mux/Cloudflare Stream signed playback token, and the EDGE would verify it —
         no origin round trip, no database, and the media never leaves the CDN's control.
         The ticket row below is the honest stand-in for that while the declared lane has no
         HMAC seam (PLAN.md §6.82); scripts/serve.mjs's /media gate is the honest stand-in
         for the edge check, and says so in place. -->
    <action as="playSource" inputs="episode">
      if (episode == null || episode === '') { throw { reason: 'invalid', message: 'episode is required' } }
      const e = await dsx.module.data.episode.get({ id: episode })
      if (!e.ok || e.data == null) { throw { reason: 'not_found', message: 'no such episode' } }
      if (e.data.state !== 'live') { throw { reason: 'not_found', message: 'no such episode' } }
      const s = await dsx.module.data.show.get({ id: e.data.show })
      if (!s.ok || s.data == null) { throw { reason: 'not_found', message: 'no such show' } }

      const free = e.data.price === 0 || e.data.idx <= s.data.free_until
      const mine = await dsx.module.data.wallet.list({ limit: 1 })
      const w = mine.ok && mine.data.length > 0 ? mine.data[0] : null
      const vip = w != null && w.vip_until != null && new Date(w.vip_until).getTime() > Date.now()
      let owned = false
      if (free === false && vip === false) {
        const u = await dsx.module.data.unlock.list({ filters: { episode: episode }, limit: 1 })
        owned = u.ok && u.data.length > 0
      }
      // FAIL CLOSED. The refusal is a declared rejection, so the client reads
      // `r.data.message` and can offer the unlock — it is not a 404 pretending the episode
      // does not exist, because the episode DOES exist and the grid already showed it.
      if (free === false && vip === false && owned === false) {
        throw { reason: 'forbidden', message: 'unlock this episode to watch it' }
      }
      if (e.data.video_url == null || e.data.video_url === '') {
        throw { reason: 'unavailable', message: 'this episode has no video yet' }
      }

      // FIVE MINUTES. Long enough that a viewer who pauses mid-episode and comes back does
      // not get a dead <video>, short enough that a leaked URL is worthless by the time it
      // is pasted anywhere. A real signed URL uses the same order of magnitude for the same
      // reason; the player re-asks on the next episode either way.
      const TTL_S = 300
      const until = new Date(Date.now() + TTL_S * 1000).toISOString()
      const have = await dsx.module.data.playticket.list({ filters: { episode: episode }, limit: 1 })
      let t = have.ok && have.data.length > 0 ? have.data[0] : null
      if (t == null) {
        const made = await dsx.module.data.playticket.create({ episode: episode, expires: until })
        if (made.ok) {
          t = made.data
        } else {
          // lost the `unique (owner_id, episode)` race with the viewer's other tab — the
          // winner's row is as good as ours, so read it rather than failing the playback
          const again = await dsx.module.data.playticket.list({ filters: { episode: episode }, limit: 1 })
          t = again.ok && again.data.length > 0 ? again.data[0] : null
        }
      } else {
        const rolled = await dsx.module.data.playticket.update({ id: t.id, values: { expires: until } })
        if (!rolled.ok) { throw { reason: 'unavailable', message: 'could not start playback' } }
      }
      if (t == null) { throw { reason: 'unavailable', message: 'could not start playback' } }

      const sep = e.data.video_url.includes('?') ? '&' : '?'
      return {
        episode: episode, idx: e.data.idx, title: e.data.title,
        source: e.data.video_url + sep + 'ep=' + episode + '&ticket=' + t.id,
        free: free, vip: vip, owned: owned, expires: until, ttl: TTL_S
      }
    </action>

    <!-- THE QUOTE. The screen has to show "unlock all 10 for 320, save 20%" before the
         viewer commits, and the price it shows must be the price it charges. Computing that
         on the client would put BULK_OFF in two places and let them drift, so the server
         quotes the same basket the same way and the UI renders what it is told. Viewer-
         specific (it subtracts what you already own), so it is auth'd, unlike the catalogue. -->
    <action as="seriesOffer" inputs="show">
      if (show == null || show === '') { throw { reason: 'invalid', message: 'show is required' } }
      const BULK_OFF = 20
      const s = await dsx.module.data.show.get({ id: show })
      if (!s.ok) { throw { reason: 'not_found', message: 'no such show' } }
      const eps = await dsx.module.data.episode.list({ filters: { show: show, state: 'live' }, limit: 100 })
      const all = eps.ok ? eps.data : []

      const mine = await dsx.module.data.wallet.list({ limit: 1 })
      const w = mine.ok && mine.data.length > 0 ? mine.data[0] : null
      const vip = w != null && w.vip_until != null && new Date(w.vip_until).getTime() > Date.now()
      const owned = await dsx.module.data.unlock.list({ filters: { show: show }, limit: 100 })
      const have = (owned.ok ? owned.data : []).map(u => u.episode)

      const chargeable = all.filter(row =>
        row.price > 0 && row.idx > s.data.free_until && !have.includes(row.id) && vip === false)
      const listTotal = chargeable.reduce((sum, row) => sum + row.price, 0)
      const total = chargeable.reduce((sum, row) => sum + Math.round(row.price * (100 - BULK_OFF) / 100), 0)
      return {
        remaining: chargeable.length,
        price: total,
        listPrice: listTotal,
        saved: listTotal - total,
        off: BULK_OFF,
        affordable: w != null && (w.coins + w.bonus) >= total
      }
    </action>

    <!-- BULK UNLOCK — "unlock all N remaining for X, save Y%". Both category leaders sell
         this and it is the single biggest basket in the funnel, so it gets the same rules as
         the one-episode path and none of the shortcuts.

         THE SERVER PRICES IT. `show` is the only input; the client never sends an amount, a
         count or a discount. BULK_OFF is a server constant, so a crafted request cannot ask
         for a better deal than the storefront offers.

         IDEMPOTENT, NOT ATOMIC — and deliberately so. Every data call runs in its OWN
         transaction (postgres.ts sets the caller identity per statement), and a declared
         action has no way to wrap several of them, so N rows for one payment cannot be made
         atomic today (PLAN.md §6.38). The fold is therefore per EPISODE — ledger, wallet,
         unlock row, one episode at a time, exactly the order the single unlock uses. A
         failure half way leaves K episodes paid for and owned and the rest untouched, which
         is consistent; re-running charges only the remainder and never twice. The tempting
         shape — debit the whole basket, then grant N rows — is the one that double-charges
         on a retry, so it is not used.

         The discount is applied to each UNIT price rather than refunded at the end: a viewer
         holding exactly the advertised total must be able to afford it, which a
         charge-full-then-credit design silently breaks. -->
    <action as="unlockSeries" inputs="show">
      if (show == null || show === '') { throw { reason: 'invalid', message: 'show is required' } }
      const BULK_OFF = 20

      const s = await dsx.module.data.show.get({ id: show })
      if (!s.ok) { throw { reason: 'not_found', message: 'no such show' } }
      // 100 is the ceiling `list` honours and never reports (PLAN.md §6.20); a series past it
      // sells what this page can see rather than pretending to sell the whole run
      const eps = await dsx.module.data.episode.list({ filters: { show: show, state: 'live' }, limit: 100 })
      const all = eps.ok ? eps.data : []

      const mine = await dsx.module.data.wallet.list({ limit: 1 })
      let w = mine.ok && mine.data.length > 0 ? mine.data[0] : null
      if (w == null) {
        // same `unique (owner_id)` re-read as walletState — two first-time calls must not
        // end up with two wallets and a split balance
        const made = await dsx.module.data.wallet.create({ coins: 0, bonus: 0 })
        if (made.ok) {
          w = made.data
        } else {
          const again = await dsx.module.data.wallet.list({ limit: 1 })
          w = again.ok && again.data.length > 0 ? again.data[0] : null
        }
        if (w == null) { throw { reason: 'unavailable', message: 'wallet unavailable' } }
      }
      const vip = w.vip_until != null && new Date(w.vip_until).getTime() > Date.now()

      const owned = await dsx.module.data.unlock.list({ filters: { show: show }, limit: 100 })
      const have = (owned.ok ? owned.data : []).map(u => u.episode)

      const chargeable = all.filter(row =>
        row.price > 0 && row.idx > s.data.free_until && !have.includes(row.id) && vip === false)
      if (chargeable.length === 0) {
        return { unlocked: 0, spent: 0, alreadyOwned: true, remaining: 0 }
      }

      const unit = (row) => Math.round(row.price * (100 - BULK_OFF) / 100)
      const total = chargeable.reduce((sum, row) => sum + unit(row), 0)
      const listTotal = chargeable.reduce((sum, row) => sum + row.price, 0)
      if (w.bonus + w.coins < total) {
        throw { reason: 'conflict', message: 'not enough coins for the full series' }
      }

      let bonusLeft = w.bonus
      let coinsLeft = w.coins
      let done = 0
      let spent = 0
      for (const row of chargeable) {
        const price = unit(row)
        // SAME LOCK, SAME ORDER as unlockEpisode: the unlock row goes in FIRST, and a
        // duplicate (this viewer's other tab running the same basket) loses the
        // `unique (owner_id, episode)` race and is SKIPPED rather than charged. This is what
        // makes "re-running charges only the remainder and never twice" true under
        // concurrency and not just under sequential retry.
        const got = await dsx.module.data.unlock.create({ episode: row.id, show: show, price: price })
        if (!got.ok) { continue }
        const fromBonus = Math.min(bonusLeft, price)
        const fromCoins = price - fromBonus
        if (fromBonus > 0) {
          const lb = await dsx.module.data.ledger.create({ kind: 'bonus', amount: -fromBonus, source: 'unlock_series', ref: row.id })
          if (!lb.ok) { throw { reason: 'unavailable', message: 'spend could not be recorded' } }
        }
        if (fromCoins > 0) {
          const lc = await dsx.module.data.ledger.create({ kind: 'coin', amount: -fromCoins, source: 'unlock_series', ref: row.id })
          if (!lc.ok) { throw { reason: 'unavailable', message: 'spend could not be recorded' } }
        }
        bonusLeft = bonusLeft - fromBonus
        coinsLeft = coinsLeft - fromCoins
        // the wallet moves WITH each episode, never once at the end: a debit that outlives a
        // failed grant is the shape that charges twice on the retry
        const paid = await dsx.module.data.wallet.update({ id: w.id, values: { bonus: bonusLeft, coins: coinsLeft } })
        if (!paid.ok) { throw { reason: 'unavailable', message: 'wallet could not be updated' } }
        done = done + 1
        spent = spent + price
      }
      return { unlocked: done, spent: spent, listPrice: listTotal, saved: listTotal - spent, remaining: 0 }
    </action>

    <!-- THE UNLOCK, AND WHY THE GRANT COMES BEFORE THE CHARGE.
         This used to read "do I already own it?", then debit, then insert the unlock row.
         Every step is its own transaction (postgres.ts sets the caller identity per
         statement; a declared action cannot wrap several — PLAN.md §6.38), so two taps that
         arrive together BOTH passed the ownership check, BOTH debited, and the viewer paid
         twice for one episode. A plain index does not stop that; only a unique constraint
         does, and the emitter has no `unique` word (PLAN.md §6.83) — so the constraint lives
         in policies.local.sql as `unique (owner_id, episode)` on dsx_unlock.
         Given the constraint, the ORDER decides which way the race fails:
           · debit → grant  ⇒ the loser is charged and gets nothing. Two charges, one episode.
           · grant → debit  ⇒ the loser is refused by the constraint and charges NOTHING.
         So the insert is moved in front of the money and IS the lock. The residual, named
         rather than hidden: if the debit fails after a successful grant, the viewer owns an
         episode they did not pay for. That is the same trade unlockSeries already documents
         and takes deliberately — losing a sale beats double-charging a customer, and it is
         the only one of the two a support ticket cannot fix. The affordability check still
         runs BEFORE the insert, so a broke viewer never gets a free grant. -->
    <action as="unlockEpisode" inputs="episode">
      if (episode == null || episode === '') { throw { reason: 'invalid', message: 'episode is required' } }
      const already = await dsx.module.data.unlock.list({ filters: { episode: episode }, limit: 1 })
      if (already.ok && already.data.length > 0) { return { unlocked: true, alreadyOwned: true } }

      const e = await dsx.module.data.episode.get({ id: episode })
      if (!e.ok || e.data == null) { throw { reason: 'not_found', message: 'no such episode' } }
      const s = await dsx.module.data.show.get({ id: e.data.show })
      if (!s.ok || s.data == null) { throw { reason: 'not_found', message: 'no such show' } }

      const mine = await dsx.module.data.wallet.list({ limit: 1 })
      let w = mine.ok && mine.data.length > 0 ? mine.data[0] : null
      if (w == null) {
        const made = await dsx.module.data.wallet.create({ coins: 0, bonus: 0 })
        if (made.ok) {
          w = made.data
        } else {
          const again = await dsx.module.data.wallet.list({ limit: 1 })
          w = again.ok && again.data.length > 0 ? again.data[0] : null
        }
        if (w == null) { throw { reason: 'unavailable', message: 'wallet unavailable' } }
      }

      const vip = w.vip_until != null && new Date(w.vip_until).getTime() > Date.now()
      const free = e.data.price === 0 || e.data.idx <= s.data.free_until
      const price = (free || vip) ? 0 : e.data.price

      // affordability BEFORE the grant, so the lock never hands out something unpaid for
      if (price > 0 && w.bonus + w.coins < price) {
        throw { reason: 'conflict', message: 'not enough coins' }
      }
      // THE LOCK. A duplicate loses here and returns without charging.
      const got = await dsx.module.data.unlock.create({ episode: episode, show: e.data.show, price: price })
      if (!got.ok) { return { unlocked: true, alreadyOwned: true, spent: 0 } }

      if (price > 0) {
        const fromBonus = Math.min(w.bonus, price)
        const fromCoins = price - fromBonus
        // ledger first, checked — the fold never moves without its rows
        if (fromBonus > 0) {
          const lb = await dsx.module.data.ledger.create({ kind: 'bonus', amount: -fromBonus, source: 'unlock', ref: episode })
          if (!lb.ok) { throw { reason: 'unavailable', message: 'spend could not be recorded' } }
        }
        if (fromCoins > 0) {
          const lc = await dsx.module.data.ledger.create({ kind: 'coin', amount: -fromCoins, source: 'unlock', ref: episode })
          if (!lc.ok) { throw { reason: 'unavailable', message: 'spend could not be recorded' } }
        }
        const paid = await dsx.module.data.wallet.update({ id: w.id, values: { bonus: w.bonus - fromBonus, coins: w.coins - fromCoins } })
        if (!paid.ok) { throw { reason: 'unavailable', message: 'wallet could not be updated' } }
      }
      return { unlocked: true, spent: price }
    </action>

    <!-- SHAPED, NOT RAW — and the reason is a live compliance leak, not tidiness.
         This used to return the row object straight off the wire. The `expires` FIELD is
         gone from the entity, but `migration.sql` is additive by construction and never
         drops a column, so an already-deployed database still HAS `dsx_ledger.expires` with
         values in it from before this pass — and pg happily returns it. The screen renders
         "· expires in 7 days" whenever that key is non-null, so a database with history
         would keep making a promise the server no longer keeps (App Store 3.1.1). Naming
         the fields the payload carries is what makes a dropped field actually gone. -->
    <action as="ledgerRows">
      const rows = await dsx.module.data.ledger.list({ limit: 100 })
      const sorted = (rows.ok ? rows.data : []).sort((a, b) => (a.created_at < b.created_at ? 1 : -1))
      return {
        rows: sorted.slice(0, 50).map(r => ({
          id: r.id, kind: r.kind, amount: r.amount, source: r.source, ref: r.ref, created_at: r.created_at
        }))
      }
    </action>
  </head>

  <!-- RATE CEILINGS. `rate` is parsed once per row at host construction and counted in
       `dsx_rate_counter` through the Postgres store (installPostgresPool fills
       RateLimitSeam.store, so the ceiling is durable rather than per-isolate — the thing
       ratelimit.ts calls "a FALSE STATEMENT in the security posture" if it is not).
       An identified caller is bucketed by `sub`, which they cannot forge; anonymous callers
       are bucketed by the address scripts/serve.mjs now supplies via `clientAddress`.
       Every number below is derived from what the SURFACE costs, not from taste:
         · state   240/m — five screens read the wallet on mount and §6.30 means a tab
                   revisit refetches; 4/s is far above a human and still caps a poller.
         · unlock   60/m — the fastest real binge is a tap a second; 60/m is 6× that and
                   caps a script at ~360 writes/min against the 500k/day write ceiling.
         · series   10/h — the biggest write in the app: 3 rows per episode, so a
                   100-episode basket is 300 writes. 10/h bounds one caller at 3,000.
         · quote    60/m — a read the Show screen re-runs as the wallet changes.
         · ledger   60/m — a page of 100 owner rows.
         · play    240/m — asked once per episode; a fast swiper legitimately runs ~60/m,
                   and the 5-minute ticket means a re-ask is cheap. 4/s bounds a scraper
                   walking a 100-episode series to ~25s per pass, which the entitlement
                   check refuses anyway. -->
  <route method="GET"  path="/wallet/state"   action="walletState"   auth="required" rate="240/m"/>
  <route method="POST" path="/wallet/unlock"  action="unlockEpisode" auth="required" rate="60/m"/>
  <route method="POST" path="/wallet/unlockseries" action="unlockSeries" auth="required" rate="10/h"/>
  <route method="GET"  path="/wallet/series/:show"  action="seriesOffer"  auth="required" rate="60/m"/>
  <route method="GET"  path="/wallet/ledger"  action="ledgerRows"    auth="required" rate="60/m"/>
  <route method="GET"  path="/wallet/play/:episode" action="playSource" auth="required" rate="240/m"/>
</server>
